44be7587ee7d6ed8c081cbb97ff7b3","sha":"1c8d709ac544be7587ee7d6ed8c081cbb97ff7b3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jagreehal/mountly/commits/1c8d709ac544be7587ee7d6ed8c081cbb97ff7b3"}],"sha":"45e067a9011837e97cf30602930514a7701d888a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jagreehal/mountly/commits/45e067a9011837e97cf30602930514a7701d888a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/3634906?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/jagreehal","id":3634906,"login":"jagreehal","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2MzQ5MDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jagreehal","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jagreehal/mountly/commits/1c8d709ac544be7587ee7d6ed8c081cbb97ff7b3/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-09T18:58:57Z","email":"jag.reehal@gmail.com","name":"Jag Reehal"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-09T18:58:57Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(embed): shadow-root distributions, and docs that lead with embeds (#268)\n\n* feat(embed): publish components as custom elements from their own types\n\nAdd script-tag component distributions. `defineElementsConfig({ prefix, elements })`\ntakes a glob of ordinary React, Vue or Svelte components, infers each one''s\nframework from its file extension, and reads its props from the source. Each\nbecomes a custom element with real attributes, real properties and real DOM\nevents — no JSON prop blob, no callback map, and nothing for the consumer to\ninstall or initialize beyond one script tag.\n\nThe build emits `embed.d.ts` (plus `embed.react.d.ts`, since React 19 resolves\nJSX from the `react` module) and a `custom-elements.json` manifest, alongside an\n`embed.js` that registers every tag and downloads nothing until an element\nconnects. Components in one distribution share their framework chunk, and a\ndistribution may mix frameworks.\n\nExtraction reads interfaces, type aliases, `extends`, intersections, literal\ntypes, quoted names, method-syntax callbacks, `memo`/`forwardRef`, Vue''s\n`defineProps`/`defineEmits`/`withDefaults` and Options API, and Svelte 5''s\n`$props`. A contract it cannot read fully fails the build rather than shipping\nan element that ignores half its props.\n\nMountly''s own controls live under `data-mountly-*` so a component may have props\nnamed `trigger` or `mount`. Attribute errors are tracked per prop, so a valid\nvalue recovers that prop while another''s error still holds.\n\nAlso fix Svelte 5 prop updates in the adapter: `mount()` reads its props object\nonce, so `update()` changed nothing on screen. The adapter now hands Svelte a\n`$state`-tracked object and patches it in place, loading the rune module on\ndemand so hosts that never mount a Svelte 5 component never pull in its runtime.\n\nDocs: script embeds are now the documented answer to sharing a component;\n`<mountly-feature>` and the import-map builds are reframed as what they are, the\nruntime for manifest-driven hosts that share one framework instance.\n\n* feat(embed): bring the framework compiler the components need\n\nVue and Svelte builds no longer name a compiler plugin. `defineElementsConfig`\nglobs the components at config time, so it already knows which frameworks are in\nplay, and puts `@vitejs/plugin-vue` or `@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte` in the\nplugin array itself — declared as optional peer dependencies, loaded only when a\nmatching file exists. A React-only build adds nothing, since oxc already handles\nJSX; that transform is now enabled only when React is actually present, so it no\nlonger tries to parse a `.vue` file first.\n\nVite resolves its plugin list before any hook runs, so this cannot be done from\nthe `config` hook. The array carries a promise instead, which `PluginOption`\nallows, keeping the function synchronous.\n\nA compiler registered twice is an error rather than a silent double transform:\nthe second copy would receive the first one''s output and fail somewhere\nunhelpful.\n\nAdds a `root` option for configs not loaded from the project root, which is what\nlets the glob run early. New `docs/examples/mixed-embed` builds React, Vue and\nSvelte components from one config with no plugins named, and the browser test\nasserts all three render and that a Svelte property update reaches its component.\n\n* feat(embed): shadow-root distributions, and docs that lead with embeds\n\n`defineElementsConfig({ shadow: true })` renders every element in a\ndistribution into its own shadow root. The build emits one stylesheet that it\ninjects into no document, and each element adopts it into its own root through\na constructable sheet shared across instances, so the host''s CSS stays out of\nthe component and the component''s CSS stays out of the host''s document. The\nsetting is distribution-wide, since it describes the kind of host you ship to.\nLight DOM stays the default: the usual consumer is a page whose design system\nshould reach in.\n\nVite writes that stylesheet from its own `generateBundle`, so the hashed name\nonly exists once every other plugin has run. The widget module carries a token\nuntil then and a post-ordered hook fills in the URL.\n\nThe docs now open on embeds. A new \"Embeds or runtime?\" page names the two\nproducts and sends each reader down one track, the sidebar groups pages the\nsame way, and the README, landing page and introduction lead with publishing a\ntag. Stage 2 of \"Choosing an architecture\" is now the script-tag embed, with\nthe import-map drop-in kept for hosts already running the runtime. Script\nembeds gain sections on the duplicate-framework cost, declaring props when the\ntype lives in another file, light DOM versus shadow, and Tailwind without\nPreflight.\n\nAdds the `publish-component-embed` skill in a new `mountly-embed` plugin, so an\nagent asked to share a component reaches for `defineElementsConfig` rather than\nwriting a custom element class. The marketplace is renamed `mountly` to match\nthe install commands the docs have always shown.\n\n`react-embed` now styles its component with Tailwind, composed onto the embed\nconfig with `mergeConfig`, importing theme and utilities but not Preflight,\nwhich would otherwise reset the consumer''s document from a light-DOM embed.\n`mixed-embed` gains a README and a runnable host page, and both are staged as\nlive examples. The examples index groups demos by track and says what each one\nshows; links there are JSX anchors, which evaluate where a markdown link\ndestination does not.\n\nTests cover shadow isolation, a hand-written props table, an embed sharing a\npage with a host that bundles its own React, and hosted example links.","tree":{"sha":"93089d6ea2f8ac5efe379b085aacab9cfcbde27c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jagreehal/mountly/git/trees/93089d6ea2f8ac5efe379b085aacab9cfcbde27c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/jagreehal/mountly/git/commits/1c8d709ac544be7587ee7d6ed8c081cbb97ff7b3","verification":{"payload":"tree 93089d6ea2f8ac5efe379b085aacab9cfcbde27c\nparent 91257fe3925f30f91c50361dc2e7b782ec47ff5c\nauthor Jag Reehal <jag.reehal@gmail.com> 1788980337 +0100\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788980337 +0100\n\nfeat(embed): shadow-root distributions, and docs that lead with embeds (#268)\n\n* feat(embed): publish components as custom elements from their own types\n\nAdd script-tag component distributions. `defineElementsConfig({ prefix, elements })`\ntakes a glob of ordinary React, Vue or Svelte components, infers each one''s\nframework from its file extension, and reads its props from the source. Each\nbecomes a custom element with real attributes, real properties and real DOM\nevents — no JSON prop blob, no callback map, and nothing for the consumer to\ninstall or initialize beyond one script tag.\n\nThe build emits `embed.d.ts` (plus `embed.react.d.ts`, since React 19 resolves\nJSX from the `react` module) and a `custom-elements.json` manifest, alongside an\n`embed.js` that registers every tag and downloads nothing until an element\nconnects. Components in one distribution share their framework chunk, and a\ndistribution may mix frameworks.\n\nExtraction reads interfaces, type aliases, `extends`, intersections, literal\ntypes, quoted names, method-syntax callbacks, `memo`/`forwardRef`, Vue''s\n`defineProps`/`defineEmits`/`withDefaults` and Options API, and Svelte 5''s\n`$props`. A contract it cannot read fully fails the build rather than shipping\nan element that ignores half its props.\n\nMountly''s own controls live under `data-mountly-*` so a component may have props\nnamed `trigger` or `mount`. Attribute errors are tracked per prop, so a valid\nvalue recovers that prop while another''s error still holds.\n\nAlso fix Svelte 5 prop updates in the adapter: `mount()` reads its props object\nonce, so `update()` changed nothing on screen. The adapter now hands Svelte a\n`$state`-tracked object and patches it in place, loading the rune module on\ndemand so hosts that never mount a Svelte 5 component never pull in its runtime.\n\nDocs: script embeds are now the documented answer to sharing a component;\n`<mountly-feature>` and the import-map builds are reframed as what they are, the\nruntime for manifest-driven hosts that share one framework instance.\n\n* feat(embed): bring the framework compiler the components need\n\nVue and Svelte builds no longer name a compiler plugin. `defineElementsConfig`\nglobs the components at config time, so it already knows which frameworks are in\nplay, and puts `@vitejs/plugin-vue` or `@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte` in the\nplugin array itself — declared as optional peer dependencies, loaded only when a\nmatching file exists. A React-only build adds nothing, since oxc already handles\nJSX; that transform is now enabled only when React is actually present, so it no\nlonger tries to parse a `.vue` file first.\n\nVite resolves its plugin list before any hook runs, so this cannot be done from\nthe `config` hook. The array carries a promise instead, which `PluginOption`\nallows, keeping the function synchronous.\n\nA compiler registered twice is an error rather than a silent double transform:\nthe second copy would receive the first one''s output and fail somewhere\nunhelpful.\n\nAdds a `root` option for configs not loaded from the project root, which is what\nlets the glob run early. New `docs/examples/mixed-embed` builds React, Vue and\nSvelte components from one config with no plugins named, and the browser test\nasserts all three render and that a Svelte property update reaches its component.\n\n* feat(embed): shadow-root distributions, and docs that lead with embeds\n\n`defineElementsConfig({ shadow: true })` renders every element in a\ndistribution into its own shadow root. The build emits one stylesheet that it\ninjects into no document, and each element adopts it into its own root through\na constructable sheet shared across instances, so the host''s CSS stays out of\nthe component and the component''s CSS stays out of the host''s document. The\nsetting is distribution-wide, since it describes the kind of host you ship to.\nLight DOM stays the default: the usual consumer is a page whose design system\nshould reach in.\n\nVite writes that stylesheet from its own `generateBundle`, so the hashed name\nonly exists once every other plugin has run. The widget module carries a token\nuntil then and a post-ordered hook fills in the URL.\n\nThe docs now open on embeds. A new \"Embeds or runtime?\" page names the two\nproducts and sends each reader down one track, the sidebar groups pages the\nsame way, and the README, landing page and introduction lead with publishing a\ntag. Stage 2 of \"Choosing an architecture\" is now the script-tag embed, with\nthe import-map drop-in kept for hosts already running the runtime. Script\nembeds gain sections on the duplicate-framework cost, declaring props when the\ntype lives in another file, light DOM versus shadow, and Tailwind without\nPreflight.\n\nAdds the `publish-component-embed` skill in a new `mountly-embed` plugin, so an\nagent asked to share a component reaches for `defineElementsConfig` rather than\nwriting a custom element class. The marketplace is renamed `mountly` to match\nthe install commands the docs have always shown.\n\n`react-embed` now styles its component with Tailwind, composed onto the embed\nconfig with `mergeConfig`, importing theme and utilities but not Preflight,\nwhich would otherwise reset the consumer''s document from a light-DOM embed.\n`mixed-embed` gains a README and a runnable host page, and both are staged as\nlive examples. The examples index groups demos by track and says what each one\nshows; links there are JSX anchors, which evaluate where a markdown link\ndestination does not.\n\nTests cover shadow isolation, a hand-written props table, an embed sharing a\npage with a host that bundles its own React, and hosted example links.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqoaxxCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAfNYQAGrsxBo5r5qvfyIqCkQFJz30\nJqRduXxoIk9QCSBJsNEZzSpwvbjSnp5kIsKGYyZEjHw4lANoJtEXkp+Z51ScVgAF\n5h4MrMOH/EBhKA/tjIyZNvomHZgTo9KhUmrSWa16IWdGJdDHJwpe+//88FzAYXPx\nM5mxcAXYVOBlyRh8G40grJbnrcJT0JgZow2kohA7qbuJrUQNJ3uyw3zRxWklv2P/\n99zL+82MpbGhoAqwRk3wOk3AiSD334u5ZDTvogf6t5rOOwFS2ftxrNL2O/DLKF